      <article class="ins-article">
        <div class="ins-source-note">
          <span class="k">In response to</span>
          <span class="body"><a href="http://www.trendforce.com/presscenter/news/20260930-13258.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AI Server Demand Keeps Memory Contract Prices Rising in 4Q26, While Consumer-Side Pressure Persists</a><span class="pub"> &middot; TrendForce</span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="ins-tldr">
          <div class="lbl">Key takeaways</div>
          <p>A market research forecast published on September 30, 2026 expects DRAM contract prices to rise 10% to 15% and NAND flash 15% to 20% in 4Q26, with AI servers absorbing supply. How long server priority lasts and how designs adapt are the open questions.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="lede">Memory contract prices are forecast to keep rising in the fourth quarter of 2026 as AI server demand absorbs DRAM and NAND flash supply, according to a <a class="inline" href="http://www.trendforce.com/presscenter/news/20260930-13258.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">market research forecast</a> published on September 30, 2026. Contract prices are the quarterly prices memory makers agree with buyers, and they set the cost of the DRAM, flash and managed storage (eMMC and UFS) fitted to printed circuit board assemblies (PCBAs).</p>
        <figure class="ins-figure"><div class="img"><img src="~/images/capabilities/cap-pcba-box-build-smt-assembly-hero.webp" alt="SMT assembly at a Hana facility"/></div></figure>
        <h2 id="what-does-the-forecast-say">What does the forecast say about memory contract prices in 4Q26?</h2>
        <p>The forecast expects conventional DRAM contract prices to rise 10% to 15% quarter over quarter in 4Q26 and overall NAND flash contract prices to rise 15% to 20%.</p>
        <ul><li>DRAM increases are expected to slow, but supply stays short because suppliers favor advanced-process capacity for server products.</li><li>Enterprise SSDs are the only category where price growth is expected to accelerate, with bit demand projected to grow more than 80% year over year in 2026.</li><li>Some server DRAM prices may trail the market average because long-term agreements (LTAs) set price ranges.</li><li>PC DRAM supply could decline in 2027 as capacity shifts to servers, and some PC brands are reducing SSD capacities in mainstream models to lower bill of materials (BOM) cost.</li></ul>
        <p>A <a class="inline" href="https://technews.tw/?p=1622115" target="_blank" rel="noopener">second published summary</a> gives the same figures. The forecast covers one quarter and may be revised.</p>
        <h2 id="how-do-rising-memory-prices">How do rising memory prices reach a PCBA bill of materials?</h2>
        <p>Memory prices reach a PCBA through its memory line items, so the effect scales with device count and density. A board with one fixed memory configuration absorbs a price change directly, while a layout that accepts two or more densities or packages gives the program room to adjust. The forecast gives no board-level cost figures, so the effect varies by program. A <a class="inline" href="/insights">separate shift in MLCC supply</a> affects the same bills of materials on boards that use both.</p>
        <h2 id="watching">What we're watching</h2>
        <p>These open questions will show how long the pressure lasts:</p>
        <div class="ins-watchlist"><ul><li>How long does server priority persist, and does the PC DRAM supply decline described for 2027 materialize?</li><li>Does enterprise SSD demand spread price growth to other NAND categories?</li><li>Do more OEMs reduce memory density in mainstream products, as some PC brands already do?</li><li>Which memory parts keep footprint-compatible alternates as capacity moves toward servers?</li></ul></div>
        <h2 id="buyers">Why it matters for manufacturing buyers</h2>
        <p>Rising memory costs will reach PCBA quotations through each bill of materials. Hana provides <a class="inline" href="/capabilities/dfx-jdm/dfx-dfm">DFx and DFM reviews</a> and SMT assembly within its <a class="inline" href="/capabilities/pcba-box-build">PCBA &amp; Box Build capability</a>, and a review can record which memory footprints and densities a layout accepts. <!-- VERIFY: DFM review scope covering memory footprint/density options is general practice, not stated in the DFx brief; Rupert to confirm wording. --> <a class="inline" href="/capabilities/automation/mes-traceability">Hana's traceability</a> covers material, unit and product, so a change of memory part can be traced to the units that carry it. Where a program dual-sources from multiple countries within the same company, each Hana site is qualified separately against the customer's requirements.</p>
